LECTURES.push({id:'l1',num:1,title:'Introduction and representation of networks',short:'Representation',rec:'Includes notes from the 2022 recording of this lecture (recording 1). You can hide the notes in the menu.',
sections:[
{id:'l1s1',title:'What a graph is, and what a network is',body:R`
<p>Network science starts from a deliberately simple object. A <dfn>graph</dfn> \(G\) consists of a set \(\mathcal N\) of \(N\) nodes (also called vertices) and a set \(\mathcal L\) of \(L\) links (also called edges), where every link joins two nodes. Unless stated otherwise the course works with a <dfn>simple graph</dfn>: links are undirected, there are no self-loops (no link from a node to itself), and two nodes are joined by at most one link. A simple graph on \(N\) nodes can therefore have at most \(\binom N2=\tfrac{N(N-1)}2\) links, one for every pair of nodes. That number comes back constantly, so it is worth recognising on sight.</p>
<voice r="1" t="5:41" hint><p>The exam as he announced it in 2022: 10 points, entirely multiple choice, generated per student from a common template so that neighbours get different questions, two hours, and open book ("the only enemy is your time"). The current exam-prep set says closed book with a formula sheet you prepare yourself, so check Brightspace for your year. What he stresses all the time: do the computations yourself. Every network on the slides is an example meant to be reproduced at home in MATLAB, Python or whatever you like; that is how you learn what an adjacency matrix really is.</p></voice>
<p>A graph on its own only tells you who is connected to whom. What makes a system interesting is what happens on those connections. A <dfn>network</dfn> is a process (the function: transport of items, flow of current, spread of a virus) running on a graph (the structure). In the Internet the graph is routers and cables and the process is packets being forwarded; in a power grid it is substations and lines, with power flowing according to Kirchhoff's laws; in the brain it is roughly \(10^{10}\) neurons with \(10^{14}\) connections (about 500,000 km of wiring, more than the distance to the moon) and the process is signalling. The same graph can carry very different processes, and the same process behaves very differently on different graphs. That duality between process and graph is the backbone of the course: Part I (lectures 1–4) studies the graph, Part II (lectures 5–6) studies a process on it, namely electrical flow.</p>
<voice r="1" t="41:47"><p>He insists on the words <em>nodes</em> and <em>links</em> rather than vertices and edges, which he calls topological creatures. Above all he stresses the duality. The structure is the part we can draw, which makes networks look easy; the service on top is what people pay for (you care about getting from A to B by train, not about the rails), and it is always some form of transport of items between nodes: electrons, packets, messages. The hard part is when process and structure interact. Rails hardly change while trains run, so engineers treat the graph as fixed to a first approximation. A virus, by contrast, spreads over a contact network that people change as soon as someone near them sneezes.</p></voice>
<h4>Why a young discipline about an old object</h4>
<p>Graphs have been studied since 1736, yet network science is only about two decades old. For a long time the graph was treated as a given constraint: telecommunication protocols were designed for whatever topology existed, and the topology of a power grid was simply fixed. In the mid-1990s measurements of the Internet and the World Wide Web showed that their degree distributions follow a power law, with a few hubs and very many poorly connected nodes, and the same law then turned up in metabolic, airline, social and citation networks. That suggested universal behaviour worth explaining. Cheap computing and the rise of data science, where relationships between items are naturally a graph, did the rest. The field is interdisciplinary (engineering, biology, physics, social science), empirical (models are tested against measured networks), mathematical (graph theory, probability, statistical physics) and computational (algorithms for graphs with millions of nodes).</p>
<voice r="1" t="24:22"><p>His own account of how the field started. When he studied, the network was simply given and you computed the currents in it. Then the Internet appeared and nobody had a map of it; the only network we know precisely, he says, is the road map. Engineers design with Gaussian error margins, yet router degrees turned out to follow a power law, a density \(f(t)\sim t^{-\alpha}\) with \(\alpha\gt1\) (otherwise it cannot be normalised), the kind of law physicists know from earthquakes and phase transitions. Power laws show up in large networks (from roughly 500 nodes on), and there is still no theory that predicts them from first principles. On computing: MATLAB handles a \(1000\times1000\) matrix easily and \(10{,}000\) sometimes, but interesting networks are far larger.</p></voice>
<h4>The first graph problem: Königsberg</h4>
<p>Euler founded graph theory with the bridges of Königsberg: can you walk through the city crossing each of its seven bridges exactly once? He replaced every land mass by a node and every bridge by a link, and asked whether the resulting graph has an <dfn>Eulerian walk</dfn>, a walk that traverses every link exactly once.</p>
<fig id="l1_konigsberg" src="L1 slide 27">Euler’s Königsberg bridge problem: an Eulerian walk exists only when all nodes have even degree or exactly two have odd degree.</fig>
<p>The argument is a counting argument on degrees (the degree of a node is its number of links). Every time the walk passes <em>through</em> a node it uses one link to arrive and another to leave, so it uses the links of that node in pairs. A node that is neither the start nor the end of the walk must therefore have even degree. Only the start and end node may have odd degree. Hence an Eulerian walk exists in a connected graph if and only if all nodes have even degree (the walk then returns to its start) or exactly two nodes have odd degree (the walk runs from one to the other). In Königsberg the island has degree 5 and the three other land masses have degree 3: four odd nodes, so no such walk exists. The question can be answered in polynomial time just by looking at degrees. Its sibling, the <dfn>Hamiltonian path</dfn> problem (visit every <em>node</em> exactly once), looks almost the same but is NP-hard; section 4 shows how the two are related through the line graph.</p>
<voice r="1" t="50:45"><p>The lesson he draws from Königsberg: Euler decided the question without computing a single route, just by noting that every visit to a land mass uses one bridge in and one out. Many graph problems are like that. With the right insight the answer is immediate; without it you compute forever, and rigorous proofs can be very hard. Mapping reality onto a mathematical structure is, in his words, the skill a university cannot teach; it comes from practice. And the contrast to remember: every link once (Eulerian) is easy, every node once (Hamiltonian) is NP-complete. "On the links it's easy, on the nodes mission impossible. Welcome to network science," as he put it.</p></voice>`},
{id:'l1s2',title:'Three representations of an undirected graph',body:R`
<p>The same undirected graph can be written down in three equivalent ways. In the <strong>topology domain</strong> it is a matrix that lists who is linked to whom, the adjacency matrix \(A\). In the <strong>spectral domain</strong> it is the set of eigenvalues and eigenvectors of that matrix, \(A=X\Lambda X^T\), with \(X\) the orthogonal matrix of eigenvectors and \(\Lambda\) the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues (lecture 3). In the <strong>geometric domain</strong> it is a simplex, a generalised triangle, in \(N-1\) dimensions (lecture 6, not examined). Nothing is lost when moving between them: each one can be rebuilt from the others, up to how the nodes are labelled.</p>
<voice r="1" t="70:35"><p>Why keep three domains? For the same reason engineers move a signal to the frequency domain to find its bandwidth: some questions are easy in one domain and hard in another. The number of triangles is a one-line spectral formula (lectures 2–3) but a counting algorithm in the topology domain, and how well a network conducts current is easiest in the geometric domain. Choose the domain that fits the problem. He keeps stressing that the decomposition \(A=X\Lambda X^T\) always exists because \(A\) is symmetric; asymmetric matrices bring Jordan forms and complications that the course avoids.</p></voice>
<p>A computer usually stores a graph differently, as a <em>list of neighbours</em> per node or as a <em>list of links</em> (the link-state database of the OSPF routing protocol is such a list). Both need about \(N+L\) entries, whereas a matrix needs \(N^2\). Since real networks are sparse (\(L\) is far smaller than \(N^2/2\)), software uses lists. Theory uses matrices, because then linear algebra does the work. The running example of the lecture has \(N=6\) nodes and \(L=9\) links (1,2), (1,3), (1,6), (2,3), (2,5), (2,6), (3,4), (4,5), (5,6); its list of neighbours starts 1: {2,3,6}, 2: {1,3,5,6}, 3: {1,2,4}.</p>
<voice r="1" t="73:35"><p>He numbers links <em>lexicographically</em> throughout the course: (1,2), (1,3), (1,6), then (2,3), (2,5), and so on, lowest first node first and then increasing second node. That is the column order of the incidence matrix, and it avoids errors when you program. The labels themselves are arbitrary: relabelling two nodes swaps two rows and two columns of \(A\), so the same graph can look completely different on paper.</p></voice>
<svg viewBox="0 0 260 160" style="max-width:260px;display:block;margin:6px 0 14px" aria-label="Example graph with six nodes and nine links"><g stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none"><path d="M60 40 L130 30 M60 40 L60 120 M60 40 L200 50 M130 30 L60 120 M130 30 L130 130 M130 30 L200 50 M60 120 L200 130 M200 130 L130 130 M130 130 L200 50"/></g><g fill="var(--accent)"><circle cx="60" cy="40" r="11"/><circle cx="130" cy="30" r="11"/><circle cx="60" cy="120" r="11"/><circle cx="200" cy="130" r="11"/><circle cx="130" cy="130" r="11"/><circle cx="200" cy="50" r="11"/></g><g fill="var(--accent-ink)" font-size="11" font-family="IBM Plex Mono,monospace" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central"><text x="60" y="40">1</text><text x="130" y="30">2</text><text x="60" y="120">3</text><text x="200" y="130">4</text><text x="130" y="130">5</text><text x="200" y="50">6</text></g></svg>
<h4>The adjacency matrix and the degree</h4>
<p>The <dfn>adjacency matrix</dfn> \(A\) is the \(N\times N\) matrix with \(a_{ij}=1\) if nodes \(i\) and \(j\) are linked and \(a_{ij}=0\) otherwise. Because links are undirected, a link between \(i\) and \(j\) shows up twice, as \(a_{ij}\) and as \(a_{ji}\), so \(A=A^T\) is symmetric. Because there are no self-loops, its diagonal is zero.</p>
<voice r="1" t="77:03" hint><p>A misconception he wants to prevent: the direction of a link is not part of the structure, it comes from the process. Current flows from high to low potential and reverses when the potential difference does. So the course only records whether a link exists (0 or 1), keeps \(A\) symmetric, and has no self-loops (a Markov chain has self-loops, but they belong to the process). Putting direction into the topology costs you spectral graph theory, because asymmetric matrices are far harder; in his words, "directed graphs: write down, way more complex".</p></voice>
<p>Two quantities can be read off \(A\) with one multiplication each. Writing \(u=(1,1,\dots,1)^T\) for the <dfn>all-one vector</dfn>, the product \(Au\) adds up every row of \(A\). Row \(i\) has a one for every neighbour of \(i\), so the row sum is the <dfn>degree</dfn> \(d_i\) of node \(i\), its number of neighbours:</p>
<div class="formula">\[d_i=\sum_{j=1}^N a_{ij},\qquad d=Au,\qquad u^TAu=\sum_{i=1}^N\sum_{j=1}^N a_{ij}=\sum_{i=1}^N d_i=2L.\]</div>
<p>The last identity says that adding up all entries of \(A\) counts every link twice (once as \(a_{ij}\), once as \(a_{ji}\)). In the running example \(d=(3,4,3,2,3,3)\) and the degrees add up to \(18=2\cdot9\). This "basic law of the degree" is the first thing to check in any exam computation.</p>
<h4>Weighted graphs</h4>
<p>An unweighted graph assumes that all nodes and all links are of the same kind. Real links differ in capacity, delay, cost or, for an electrical network, conductance. A <dfn>weighted adjacency matrix</dfn> keeps the structure of \(A\) and attaches a number to each link: \(\widetilde A=W\circ A\), where \(W\) holds the link weights and \(\circ\) is the Hadamard product (multiply entry by entry), so that a weight survives only where a link exists. When a link has several properties at once, as in quality-of-service routing, its entry becomes a vector of weights. Lecture 5 uses exactly this with weights \(1/r_{ij}\), the conductances of resistors.</p>
<voice r="1" t="94:50"><p>In his notation \(A\) is always the 0/1 matrix and \(W\) carries the weights. Weights are positive in this course; negative weights cause trouble with cycles. Examples he gives for a telecom link: capacity, delay, packet loss, the cost an operator charges, CPU power.</p></voice>`},
{id:'l1s2b',title:'Incidence matrix and Laplacian',body:R`
<p>The adjacency matrix records links between pairs of nodes. For flows we also need to know which node is at which end of a link and in which direction something moves. The <dfn>incidence matrix</dfn> provides that. Give every link an arbitrary direction and number the links \(l_1,\dots,l_L\). Then \(B\) is the \(N\times L\) matrix whose column \(k\), belonging to link \(l_k=(i,j)\) directed from \(i\) to \(j\), contains \(b_{ik}=+1\) at the source, \(b_{jk}=-1\) at the destination, and zeros elsewhere.</p>
<p>Every column contains exactly one \(+1\) and one \(-1\), so every column sums to zero, which is the statement \(B^Tu=0\). The row of node \(i\) has a non-zero entry for each of its \(d_i\) links. The directions are a bookkeeping convention: they tell you how to read the sign of a flow, not which way it physically goes. In lecture 5 the same matrix expresses Kirchhoff's current law as \(By=x\) and the potential difference over every link as \(B^Tv\).</p>
<voice r="1" t="86:13" hint><p>He asked this in class as an "obvious" question: every column of \(B\) holds one \(+1\) (source) and one \(-1\) (destination), so every column sums to zero, \(u^TB=0\). "Never forget it," he said, "write it down on your formula list." That is the formula sheet you are allowed to bring.</p></voice>
<fig id="l1_inc" src="L1 slide 33">Incidence matrix: +1 at the source, −1 at the destination of each link; columns sum to zero.</fig>
<h4>The Laplacian</h4>
<p>Multiplying the incidence matrix by its transpose gives the most important matrix of the course, the <dfn>Laplacian</dfn> \(Q=BB^T\). It is worth computing it entry by entry once. The diagonal entry \((BB^T)_{ii}=\sum_k b_{ik}^2\) counts the links that touch node \(i\), because each contributes \((\pm1)^2=1\); that is the degree \(d_i\). An off-diagonal entry \((BB^T)_{ij}=\sum_k b_{ik}b_{jk}\) is non-zero only for a link that has both \(i\) and \(j\) as end points, and then one of the two entries is \(+1\) and the other \(-1\), giving \(-1\). So</p>
<div class="formula">\[Q=BB^T=\Delta-A,\qquad \Delta=\operatorname{diag}(d_1,d_2,\dots,d_N),\qquad Qu=B\,(B^Tu)=0.\]</div>
<p>Three consequences follow directly. First, \(Q\) does not depend on the directions chosen for \(B\): flipping a link multiplies a column of \(B\) by \(-1\), which cancels in \(BB^T\). The Laplacian, like \(A\), describes an undirected graph. Second, \(Qu=0\): every row of \(Q\) sums to zero (the degree on the diagonal cancels the \(-1\)'s of the neighbours), so \(u\) is an eigenvector of \(Q\) with eigenvalue \(\mu=0\) and \(Q\) is singular, \(\det Q=0\). Lecture 5 has to work around exactly this when it tries to invert \(x=Qv\). Third, for any vector \(z\),</p>
<div class="formula">\[z^TQz=z^TBB^Tz=\|B^Tz\|^2=\sum_{\text{links }l=(i,j)}\left(z_i-z_j\right)^2\ \ge 0.\]</div>
<p>The quadratic form adds up, over all links, the squared difference of \(z\) across the link. It can never be negative, so \(Q\) is positive semi-definite and all its eigenvalues are non-negative, and it is zero exactly when \(z\) is constant on each connected piece of the graph. This one identity powers spectral partitioning in lecture 3 (with \(z\) a \(\pm1\) vector it counts cut links) and power dissipation in lecture 6 (with \(z\) the potentials it is the dissipated power).</p>
<fig id="l1_lap" src="L1 slide 34">Laplacian Q = BBᵀ = Δ − A with Qu = 0.</fig>
<voice r="1" t="90:54" hint><p>His deeper reading of \(Qu=0\): \(u\) is an eigenvector of \(Q\) with eigenvalue 0, a direct bridge between the topology and the spectral domain. Finding eigenvectors is normally hard, but here the structure hands you one eigenvalue and its eigenvector for free, one reason he ranks \(Q\) above \(A\). It also means \(Q\) is singular, which lecture 5 has to deal with. And a warning he repeats: \(Q=BB^T\) is symmetric even though \(B\) carries directions, so never build an asymmetric "Laplacian" from a directed adjacency matrix; "conceptually you will hit the walls".</p></voice>
<div class="note">Exam prep Q8 asks which vector \(z\) satisfies \(Qz=0\) and what that implies for the rows of \(Q\): \(z=u\), and every row sums to zero, \(d_i=\sum_j a_{ij}\). Homework 1 A.3 reconstructs a Laplacian from a few entries using only these facts: \(Q\) is symmetric, off-diagonal entries are \(0\) or \(-1\), and rows sum to zero.</div>
<p>The lab below lets you build graphs and watch these quantities change. Try the lecture example, then delete a link and see the degree vector and the link count follow the basic law.</p>
<ix id="graphlab" title="Graph lab: edit a graph and watch its matrices and metrics"></ix>`},
{id:'l1s3',title:'Classes of graphs and counting graphs',body:R`
<p>A handful of graph families return throughout the course because their properties can be computed by hand. They serve as test cases: when you derive a formula, check it on these first.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table><tr><th>Family</th><th>Links \(L\)</th><th>Degrees</th><th>Remember it for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tree</td><td>\(N-1\)</td><td>vary</td><td>one path between any two nodes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Star \(K_{1,N-1}\)</td><td>\(N-1\)</td><td>\(N-1\) and \(1\)</td><td>largest betweenness, \(\lambda_1=\sqrt{N-1}\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Path \(P_N\)</td><td>\(N-1\)</td><td>\(2\) inside, \(1\) at the ends</td><td>diameter \(N-1\)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ring \(C_N\)</td><td>\(N\)</td><td>all \(2\)</td><td>two routes between any pair</td></tr>
<tr><td>Complete \(K_N\)</td><td>\(\binom N2\)</td><td>all \(N-1\)</td><td>contains every graph on \(N\) nodes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Complete bipartite \(K_{m,n}\)</td><td>\(mn\)</td><td>\(n\) and \(m\)</td><td>no odd cycles, symmetric spectrum</td></tr></table></div>
<p>A <dfn>tree</dfn> is a connected graph without cycles. Building one node by node, each new node needs exactly one link to join the existing tree: none and it is disconnected, two and it closes a cycle. Starting from a single node this gives \(L=N-1\), the minimum for any connected graph. Two trees deserve their own names: the <dfn>star</dfn> \(K_{1,N-1}\), one centre linked to \(N-1\) leaves, and the <dfn>path</dfn> \(P_N\), a single chain. The <dfn>ring</dfn> \(C_N\) closes the path into a cycle, so \(L=N\) and every node has degree 2. In the <dfn>complete graph</dfn> \(K_N\) every pair is linked, \(L=\binom N2\). It is sometimes called the mother of all graphs, because every graph on \(N\) nodes is obtained from \(K_N\) by deleting links, a fact used in lecture 3 to bound eigenvalues.</p>
<voice r="1" t="97:53"><p>Why trees are easy, in his words: no cycles, so exactly one path between any two nodes. And they are extremal, connecting all nodes with the fewest links. His quick test: if a graph is connected and has fewer links than nodes, it must be a tree. Learn \(L=\binom N2\) for the complete graph by heart; the binomial notation comes back constantly. Two side remarks: a lattice is regular except at its border (degree 4 inside, 3 on the edges, 2 in the corners), and the course only considers finite graphs.</p></voice>
<p>A <dfn>regular graph</dfn> has the same degree \(r\) at every node, so \(d=ru\) and, by the basic law, \(L=Nr/2\). Since \(L\) must be an integer, \(Nr\) has to be even: there is no 3-regular graph on 5 nodes. The ring is 2-regular and \(K_N\) is \((N-1)\)-regular. A <dfn>complete bipartite graph</dfn> \(K_{m,n}\) splits the nodes into two groups of \(m\) and \(n\) and links every node of one group to every node of the other, with no links inside a group; hence \(L=mn\). Any walk alternates between the groups, so a bipartite graph has no cycle of odd length, which is why its eigenvalues come in pairs \(\pm\lambda\) (lecture 3). The star is \(K_{1,N-1}\). For completeness: the null graph has no links at all (\(A=O\)), and in a \(D\)-dimensional lattice every interior node has \(2D\) neighbours.</p>
<fig id="l1_classes" src="L1 slide 37">Trees (L = N−1), the ring (L = N) and the complete graph (L = N(N−1)/2).</fig>
<h4>How many graphs are there?</h4>
<p>With the nodes labelled \(1,\dots,N\), there are \(M=\binom N2\) possible links, and every one of them is either present or absent. That gives \(2^{M}=2^{N(N-1)/2}\) labelled graphs on \(N\) nodes. If the number of links is fixed at \(L\), a graph is fixed by choosing which \(L\) of the \(M\) possible links are present, so there are \(\binom ML\) of them. For \(N=4\): \(M=6\), there are \(2^6=64\) labelled graphs, and \(\binom65=6\) of them have five links.</p>
<p>Counting <em>unlabelled</em> graphs is harder. Two labelled graphs are the same unlabelled graph if relabelling the nodes turns one into the other. A relabelling is described by a permutation matrix \(P\) (one 1 in every row and column, \(P^T=P^{-1}\)), and it changes the adjacency matrix into \(P^TAP\). Graphs related in this way are called <dfn>isomorphic</dfn>. Of the six labelled graphs on 4 nodes with 5 links, all are \(K_4\) minus one link, and any missing link can be moved to any other by relabelling, so they are a single unlabelled graph. In total there are 11 unlabelled graphs on 4 nodes. For large \(N\) almost every graph has no symmetry, so each unlabelled graph appears in about \(N!\) labelled versions, and the number of unlabelled graphs is roughly \(2^{M}/N!\).</p>
<div class="note">Exam prep Q11–12: \(\binom{N(N-1)/2}{L}=\binom65=6\) labelled graphs with \(N=4,L=5\), and only 1 unlabelled one. The lecture's exercise "write the permutation matrix that swaps labels 3 and 6" is the identity matrix with rows 3 and 6 interchanged.</div>`},
{id:'l1s4',title:'Complement and line graph',body:R`
<p>Two transformations build a new graph from an old one, and both are used to turn a hard question into an easier one.</p>
<h4>The complement</h4>
<p>The <dfn>complement</dfn> \(G^c\) has a link exactly where \(G\) has none. In matrix form, start from the all-one matrix \(J=uu^T\), which links every pair including each node to itself, remove the diagonal by subtracting \(I\), and remove the existing links by subtracting \(A\):</p>
<div class="formula">\[A^c=J-I-A,\qquad L^c=\binom N2-L.\]</div>
<p>The complement of \(K_N\) is the null graph, the complement of the ring \(C_5\) is again a \(C_5\), and the complement of a random graph in which each link is present with probability \(p\) is a random graph with probability \(1-p\) (lecture 4). The complement is useful because some properties are easier to see there: if \(G\) is disconnected, \(G^c\) is connected (lecture 2), and the Laplacian eigenvalues of \(G\) and \(G^c\) mirror each other, \(\mu_k(G^c)=N-\mu_{N-k}(G)\) (lecture 3, used in Exam prep Q9).</p>
<h4>The line graph</h4>
<p>The <dfn>line graph</dfn> \(l(G)\) swaps the roles of nodes and links. Every link of \(G\) becomes a node of \(l(G)\), and two nodes of \(l(G)\) are linked when the corresponding links of \(G\) share an end point. A star with three leaves, for example, has three links that all meet at the centre, so its line graph is a triangle; in general the line graph of the star \(K_{1,N-1}\) is the complete graph \(K_{N-1}\).</p>
<fig id="l1_linegraph" src="L1 slide 40">Original graph and its line graph: link properties of G become node properties of l(G).</fig>
<p>The line graph has a compact matrix description in terms of the <dfn>unsigned incidence matrix</dfn> \(R=|B|\), the incidence matrix with all signs made positive. Each row of \(R\) has a one for every link of the node and each column has two ones (the two ends of the link), so</p>
<div class="formula">\[Ru=d,\qquad R^Tu=2u,\qquad RR^T=\Delta+A,\qquad R^TR=2I+A_{l(G)}.\]</div>
<p>The third identity is the computation that gave \(Q=BB^T\), except that without the minus signs the off-diagonal entries become \(+1\); it gives back \(A=RR^T-\Delta\). The fourth is the same computation in the other order. \(R^TR\) is an \(L\times L\) matrix whose diagonal entry is 2 (every link has two ends) and whose off-diagonal entry is 1 when two links share a node, which is exactly the adjacency of the line graph. Hence \(A_{l(G)}=R^TR-2I\).</p>
<p>Counting with this matrix gives the size of the line graph. Its number of links is half the sum of its entries:</p>
<div class="formula">\[L_{l(G)}=\tfrac12u^TA_{l(G)}u=\tfrac12\left(u^TR^TRu-2L\right)=\tfrac12\,d^Td-L=\sum_{i=1}^N\binom{d_i}{2},\]</div>
<p>using \(Ru=d\). The right-hand side has a direct meaning: the \(d_i\) links meeting at node \(i\) are pairwise adjacent in \(l(G)\), so each node of \(G\) turns into a clique of \(\binom{d_i}2\) links. In the same way, the link \((i,j)\) of \(G\) has degree \(d_i+d_j-2\) in the line graph (the other links at \(i\) plus the other links at \(j\)), which is why the line graph of an \(r\)-regular graph is \(2(r-1)\)-regular. The line graph has \(L\) nodes, so it can be larger than \(G\) (usually) or smaller (a tree has \(L=N-1\)).</p>
<p>Every graph has a line graph, but not every graph is a line graph: the star with three leaves is not the line graph of anything. When a graph is a line graph, the original can be recovered in polynomial time (the ILIGRA algorithm). The practical use is that properties of links in \(G\) become properties of nodes in \(l(G)\): a strategy to protect or attack nodes in \(l(G)\) is a strategy for links in \(G\), and node metrics such as betweenness can be applied to links this way.</p>
<voice r="1" t="103:24"><p>He calls the line graph cool and gives it as homework: draw a few line graphs yourself. His reason it matters: a property proven for all nodes carries over to all links by applying it to the line graph, where the familiar square-matrix tools work (the incidence matrix, the other link-based description, is rectangular). On the complement he admits he does not really consider the empty graph a graph, since no process can run on it, but it is included for completeness as the complement of \(K_N\).</p></voice>
<p>The line graph also explains the link between the two walk problems of section 1. An Eulerian walk in \(G\) runs through every link once, each link sharing a node with the next, so it is a path through every node of \(l(G)\): a Hamiltonian path. Since Eulerian walks are found in polynomial time, have we solved an NP-hard problem? No. The correspondence only applies to graphs that are line graphs, and even there it runs one way: in \(l(G)\) three links meeting at one node are mutually adjacent, so a Hamiltonian path can jump between them in an order that no walk in \(G\) can follow.</p>
<div class="note">Exam prep Q5 compares node betweenness in \(l(G)\) with link betweenness in \(G\). Exercise session 1, Problem 4 works out \(l(K_{m,n})\): \(L_{l}=m\binom n2+n\binom m2\), its triangles, and its betweenness.</div>`},
{id:'l1s5',title:'Subgraphs, interdependent and temporal networks',body:R`
<p>A subgraph \(S\) is a subset of the nodes together with the links among them. The node labels are arbitrary, so we may relabel the nodes such that those of \(S\) come first. A relabelling permutes the rows and the columns of \(A\) in the same way (\(A\to P^TAP\)), and after it the adjacency matrix falls apart into blocks:</p>
<div class="formula">\[A=\begin{pmatrix}A_S & C\\ C^T & A_{G\setminus S}\end{pmatrix}.\]</div>
<p>The diagonal blocks are the adjacency matrices of \(S\) and of the rest of the graph. The off-diagonal block \(C\) (written \(B\) on the slides, not to be confused with the incidence matrix) is the interconnection matrix: \(c_{ij}=1\) when node \(i\) of \(S\) is linked to node \(j\) outside \(S\). It is generally not square, and since \(A\) is symmetric the lower block is \(C^T\). Adding up its entries, \(u^TCu\), counts the links that cross between \(S\) and the rest, the size of the cut. In the running example, \(S=\{1,2,6\}\) is a triangle (3 internal links), the rest \(\{3,4,5\}\) has the links 3–4 and 4–5, and the cut contains 1–3, 2–3, 2–5 and 5–6, so \(u^TCu=4\) and indeed \(3+2+4=9=L\). This block form is the starting point for the interlacing theorem and for computing spectra with the Schur complement in lecture 3.</p>
<voice r="1" t="107:14"><p>Why the cut set matters, with his Corona example: if everyone in one group is infected, the virus can only reach the other group over the links of the cut. All other links are irrelevant for that step of the spread, and a larger cut means a larger threat. He also admitted that the first block-matrix example on the slides (two groups of three nodes) is not a good one, because all blocks are square there; the second example shows the rectangular off-diagonal block.</p></voice>
<h4>Interdependent networks</h4>
<p>Most infrastructures do not work in isolation. A telecommunication network needs electricity, and the power grid is controlled through a communication network. Networks that need each other to function form an <dfn>interdependent network</dfn>. Its adjacency matrix has the same block structure: the two networks on the diagonal and a dependence matrix \(B\) off the diagonal that says which node of one network relies on which node of the other, and how strongly.</p>
<div class="formula">\[A=\begin{pmatrix}A_{G_A} & B\\ B^T & A_{G_B}\end{pmatrix},\qquad\text{simplest case: } B=pI.\]</div>
<p>In the simplest case every node depends on its counterpart in the other network with the same strength \(p\). The hard part in practice is determining \(B\) at all. The block form matters because a failure in one layer can propagate through \(B\) into the other, and back again.</p>
<fig id="l1_interdep" src="L1 slide 46">Two interdependent networks: the dependence matrix B couples the two adjacency matrices.</fig>
<h4>Temporal networks</h4>
<p>Many networks change over time. Who meets whom in a school or a train changes every few minutes, and an epidemic spreads over those changing contacts, not over a fixed graph. A <dfn>temporal graph</dfn> is a sequence of adjacency matrices \(A(t_1),A(t_2),\dots,A(t_K)\) on the same set of nodes. Usually a process generates the sequence, for instance people moving between locations. Lecture 4 builds such a model (random walkers on a map, where walkers at the same place are linked) and shows that it can be analysed and even inverted from data.</p>
<voice r="1" t="109:43"><p>He calls interdependent and temporal networks simply difficult: that is where the research is. A temporal network needs a process that generates it, and even during the Corona crisis there was no realistic model of how people change their contacts. The last slides of the lecture are puzzles and open problems; he asks you to read them.</p></voice>`}
],
defs:[
{t:'Graph',d:R`Set \(\mathcal N\) of \(N\) nodes connected by a set \(\mathcal L\) of \(L\) links.`},
{t:'Simple graph',d:R`Undirected graph without self-loops and with at most one link per node pair; at most \(\binom N2\) links.`},
{t:'Network',d:R`Process (function) + graph (structure); network science is the theory of processes on graphs.`},
{t:'Eulerian walk',d:R`Walk that traverses every link exactly once; exists in a connected graph iff all degrees are even or exactly two are odd.`},
{t:'Hamiltonian path',d:R`Path that visits every node exactly once; deciding whether one exists is NP-hard.`},
{t:'Adjacency matrix \\(A\\)',d:R`\(N\times N\) symmetric 0–1 matrix, \(a_{ij}=1\) iff \(i\sim j\); \(d=Au\); \(u^TAu=2L\).`},
{t:'Degree \\(d_i\\)',d:R`Number of neighbours of node \(i\): \(d_i=\sum_j a_{ij}\).`},
{t:'All-one vector \\(u\\)',d:R`\(u=(1,1,\dots,1)^T\); \(J=uu^T\) is the all-one matrix.`},
{t:'Incidence matrix \\(B\\)',d:R`\(N\times L\); column of link \((i,j)\) has \(+1\) at source \(i\), \(-1\) at destination \(j\); \(B^Tu=0\).`},
{t:'Laplacian \\(Q\\)',d:R`\(Q=BB^T=\Delta-A\); symmetric, positive semi-definite (\(z^TQz=\sum_{\text{links}}(z_i-z_j)^2\)), \(Qu=0\), rows sum to zero.`},
{t:'Weighted adjacency matrix',d:R`\(\widetilde A=W\circ A\) (Hadamard product of link-weight matrix \(W\) and 0–1 adjacency \(A\)).`},
{t:'Tree',d:R`Connected graph without cycles; \(L=N-1\). Star and path are trees.`},
{t:'Star \\(K_{1,N-1}\\)',d:R`Tree with one centre node linked to all \(N-1\) leaves; \(\lambda_1=\sqrt{N-1}\), line graph \(K_{N-1}\).`},
{t:'Path \\(P_N\\)',d:R`Tree whose nodes form a single chain; \(L=N-1\), diameter \(N-1\).`},
{t:'Ring \\(C_N\\)',d:R`Cycle on \(N\) nodes, \(L=N\), 2-regular.`},
{t:'Complete graph \\(K_N\\)',d:R`Every pair linked, \(L=N(N-1)/2\), \((N-1)\)-regular.`},
{t:'Regular graph',d:R`All nodes have the same degree \(r\); \(d=ru\), \(L=Nr/2\) (so \(Nr\) is even).`},
{t:'Complete bipartite \\(K_{m,n}\\)',d:R`Two sets of sizes \(m,n\); all cross links, no internal links; \(L=mn\); no odd cycles.`},
{t:'Complement \\(G^c\\)',d:R`Link present in \(G^c\) iff absent in \(G\): \(A^c=J-I-A\), \(L^c=\binom N2-L\).`},
{t:'Line graph \\(l(G)\\)',d:R`Nodes of \(l(G)\) are links of \(G\); adjacent if the links share a node. \(A_{l(G)}=R^TR-2I\), \(L_{l(G)}=\tfrac12 d^Td-L=\sum_i\binom{d_i}2\).`},
{t:'Unsigned incidence matrix \\(R\\)',d:R`\(R=|B|\); \(Ru=d\), \(R^Tu=2u\), \(RR^T=\Delta+A\), \(R^TR=2I+A_{l(G)}\).`},
{t:'Interdependent network',d:R`Networks that depend on each other to function; block adjacency with dependence matrix \(B\) off the diagonal.`},
{t:'Temporal graph',d:R`Sequence of adjacency matrices \(A(t_k)\) on the same node set, changing over time.`}
]});

LECTURES.push({id:'l2',num:2,title:'Graph metrics',short:'Metrics',rec:'Includes notes from the 2022 recording of this lecture (recording 2). Closeness, modularity, Katz centrality, coreness and the friendship paradox are only on the newer slides, so those parts have no notes from class. You can hide the notes in the menu.',
sections:[
{id:'l2s1',title:'Degree: laws, bounds and graphical sequences',body:R`
<p>The adjacency matrix contains everything there is to know about a graph, but a table of \(N^2\) zeros and ones is not insight. A <dfn>graph metric</dfn> condenses one property into a number, or into one number per node or link. Some metrics are local, describing the neighbourhood of a node (degree, clustering), and others are global, describing the whole graph (diameter, connectivity). Centrality metrics form an important subgroup: they rank how important a node or link is, for instance to find the most influential spreader for a marketing campaign or the node whose failure hurts the network most. Hundreds of metrics have been proposed (a catalogue lists about 400). This lecture covers the standard ones: degree, clustering, hopcount and diameter, betweenness, connectivity, assortativity and modularity. Spectral metrics follow in lecture 3 and the effective resistance in lecture 5.</p>
<voice r="2" t="2:45"><p>He starts from the premise that the adjacency matrix or the Laplacian is known and contains the whole graph; a metric is then something you compute from it. He separates two kinds. Some metrics are mathematically exact (the degree, the number of triangles). Others are definitions made by people (the clustering coefficient): once everyone uses the same definition the work is precise, but you can always ask whether it is a good definition, and that is where network science becomes debatable. Centrality metrics are popular because they link structure to function: which nodes are influential, and which node or link removal hurts the network most, for instance by disconnecting it.</p></voice>
<h4>The basic law of the degree</h4>
<p>The degree \(d_j\) of node \(j\) is its number of neighbours. Every link has two end points and adds one to the degree of each, so adding up all degrees counts every link twice:</p>
<div class="formula">\[\sum_{j=1}^N d_j=u^Td=u^TAu=2L.\]</div>
<p>The average degree follows immediately, \(d_{av}=\tfrac1N\sum_j d_j=\tfrac{2L}N\). Seen as a random variable, \(D\) is the degree of a node chosen uniformly at random, and \(E[D]=\tfrac{2L}N\). A connected graph needs at least \(N-1\) links (a tree) and has at most \(\binom N2\) (the complete graph), which bounds the average degree:</p>
<div class="formula">\[2-\frac2N\ \le\ E[D]\ \le\ N-1\qquad\text{for a connected graph.}\]</div>
<voice r="2" t="8:28"><p>He calls \(N\) and \(L\) the <em>parameters</em> of a graph rather than properties, and gives one rule "for the rest of your career": never compare networks with a different number of nodes. Their matrices live in spaces of different dimension, so the comparison has no common ground. The lower bound is reached by a tree, the only kind of connected graph with as few as \(N-1\) links, and the upper bound by the complete graph. In matrix form the degree law is a quadratic form: \(u^TMu\) adds up all entries of any matrix \(M\), and quadratic forms return in lecture 3 because eigenvalues can be written as quadratic forms too. The degree can also be read from a diagonal, \(d_j=(A^2)_{jj}\).</p></voice>
<p>Two facts that look like puzzles follow from simple counting. <strong>At least two nodes have the same degree</strong> (for \(N\gt1\)). The degrees can take the \(N\) values \(0,1,\dots,N-1\), but \(0\) and \(N-1\) cannot both occur: a node of degree \(N-1\) is linked to every other node, so no node can be isolated. That leaves at most \(N-1\) values for \(N\) nodes, and two nodes must share one (the pigeonhole principle). <strong>The number of nodes with odd degree is even.</strong> Split the degree sum into the odd-degree and even-degree nodes: \(\sum_{\text{odd}}d_j=2L-\sum_{\text{even}}d_j\), which is even. A sum of odd numbers is even only when it has an even number of terms.</p>
<voice r="2" t="16:27" hint><p>Two things he asks you to remember. The largest possible degree in a graph with \(N\) nodes is \(N-1\) ("never forget it"). And because of the pigeonhole argument, a candidate degree vector whose entries are all different can never belong to a graph: a quick first check before any theorem. Deciding in general whether a list is graphical sounds innocent, but he warns that the full answer is surprisingly difficult.</p></voice>
<div class="note">Homework 1 A.2 is built on the first fact. With \(N=7\), all-distinct degrees would have to be \((6,5,4,3,2,1,0)\), impossible because a degree-6 node excludes a degree-0 node. If exactly one pair shares a degree, one of the values \(0\) and \(6\) is missing and the other occurs exactly once.</div>
<h4>The degree distribution</h4>
<p>The <dfn>degree distribution</dfn> \(\Pr[D=k]\) is the fraction of nodes with degree \(k\): the histogram of the degree vector, normalised by \(N\). Its mean is \(E[D]=2L/N\). Many real networks have a <dfn>power-law degree distribution</dfn>, \(\Pr[D=k]\approx ck^{-\tau}\): plotted on log–log axes the distribution is close to a straight line with slope \(-\tau\). Such networks contain a few hubs with very large degree alongside very many nodes with small degree, and are called scale-free (lecture 4 explains the name and why \(\tau\) usually lies between 2 and 3). The US airline network has \(\tau\approx1.2\) and the Internet \(\tau\in(2.2,2.5)\).</p>
<voice r="2" t="26:04" hint><p>The histogram of the degree vector, normalised, is the distribution, and its mean must come out as \(2L/N\): a check he wants you to be able to do. The standard deviation then tells how far the degrees spread around that mean. For the power law he insists on the exact form behind the "\(\approx\)" (he writes the exponent as \(\alpha\)): \(\Pr[D=k]=ck^{-\tau}\), with \(c\) a normalisation constant fixed by \(\sum_{k=1}^{N-1}ck^{-\tau}=1\). The sum starts at \(k=1\) because a connected graph has no node of degree 0.</p></voice>
<fig id="l2_airline" src="L2 slide 9">Airline transportation network: a power-law (scale-free) degree distribution.</fig>
<h4>Which degree vectors are possible?</h4>
<p>Given a list of integers, is there a simple graph with exactly these degrees? If so the list is called <dfn>graphical</dfn>. An even sum is necessary but not sufficient. For \((3,3,1,1)\) the sum is 8, yet on 4 nodes the two nodes of degree 3 are linked to everyone, which gives the other two nodes degree at least 2: not graphical. Sort the list in non-increasing order, \(d_1\ge d_2\ge\dots\ge d_N\). Two tests decide the question.</p>
<p>The <strong>Erdős–Gallai theorem</strong> says the list is graphical if and only if the sum is even and, for every \(k=1,\dots,N\),</p>
<div class="formula">\[\sum_{j=1}^k d_j\ \le\ k(k-1)+\sum_{j=k+1}^N\min(d_j,k).\]</div>
<p>The inequality reads naturally. The left side is the total degree of the \(k\) best-connected nodes. Those link ends either go to another node of the group, which uses at most \(k(k-1)\) of them (every pair linked, counted twice), or to a node \(j\) outside the group, which can absorb at most \(\min(d_j,k)\) of them (it has \(d_j\) link ends and at most one link to each of the \(k\) nodes). For \((4,3,3,2,1,1)\) the left and right sides are \(4\le5\), \(7\le8\), \(10\le10\), \(12\le14\), \(13\le21\), \(14\le30\): graphical. For \((3,3,1,1)\) the case \(k=2\) gives \(6\le2+1+1=4\), which fails.</p>
<p>The <strong>Havel–Hakimi theorem</strong> is constructive. Connect the node of largest degree \(d_1\) (the hub) to the \(d_1\) nodes with the next-largest degrees, remove the hub, and subtract one from the degrees of those nodes; the original list is graphical if and only if the reduced list is. For \((4,3,3,2,1,1)\): linking the hub to the nodes of degree 3, 3, 2, 1 leaves \((2,2,1,0,1)\), sorted \((2,2,1,1,0)\); the next hub leaves \((1,0,1,0)\), sorted \((1,1,0,0)\); one more step leaves \((0,0,0)\). Every step succeeded, so the list is graphical, and the links added along the way form a graph that realises it. (The slides keep the nodes in their original positions, writing the same steps as \((0,2,2,1,0,1)\to(0,0,1,0,0,1)\to0\).) Choosing a different hub at each step produces different graphs with the same degrees; how many graphs share a degree vector is a hard, partly open problem.</p>
<fig id="l2_havel" src="L2 slide 11">Checking graphicality of (4,3,3,2,1,1) with Erdős–Gallai and the Havel–Hakimi reduction.</fig>
<p>Degrees also determine part of the line graph from lecture 1: link \((i,j)\) has degree \(d_i+d_j-2\) in \(l(G)\), and \(l(G)\) has \(\tfrac12d^Td-L\) links. The first formula shows that two graphs with the same degree vector can still have line graphs with different degree vectors, depending on which degrees are joined by links (Exercise session 1, Problem 4f).</p>`},
{id:'l2s2',title:'Clustering coefficient and transitivity',body:R`
<p>In friendship networks your friends tend to know each other. The clustering coefficient measures this local density: how many of the possible links among the neighbours of a node actually exist.</p>
<p>Take a node \(v\) with \(d_v\) neighbours. Among them there can be at most \(y_{\max}=\binom{d_v}2\) links. If \(y\) of them are present, the <dfn>clustering coefficient of a node</dfn> is</p>
<div class="formula">\[c_G(v)=\frac{y}{y_{\max}}=\frac{y}{\binom{d_v}2}=\frac{2y}{d_v(d_v-1)}\in[0,1],\qquad c_G(v)=0\ \text{if } d_v\le1.\]</div>
<p>Every link between two neighbours of \(v\) closes a triangle through \(v\), so \(y\) is also the number of triangles at \(v\). On the slide, node \(a\) has 4 neighbours (6 possible pairs) of which one pair is linked, so \(c_G(a)=1/6\); a node with 6 neighbours and 4 linked pairs has \(c_G(v)=\tfrac{2\cdot4}{6\cdot5}=\tfrac4{15}\). The <dfn>clustering coefficient of the graph</dfn> is the plain average over all nodes, \(c_G=\tfrac1N\sum_v c_G(v)\).</p>
<fig id="l2_cluster" src="L2 slide 17">Local clustering coefficient: links among the neighbours of v divided by the possible pairs.</fig>
<voice r="2" t="28:36"><p>To compute \(c_G(v)\) he draws the neighbours of \(v\) on a circle around it and counts the links among them. The maximum, \(c_G(v)=1\), means the neighbours form a clique: a complete graph once \(v\) itself is left out. A node with a single neighbour gets \(c_G(v)=0\) by agreement, since the formula would divide by zero.</p></voice>
<h4>Transitivity: a second, spectral definition</h4>
<p>The second definition counts globally: the fraction of connected triples (paths of two links \(i-j-k\)) that close into a triangle. This is the <dfn>transitivity</dfn>, also called the geometrical clustering coefficient, and it has a neat expression in terms of walks and eigenvalues. Each triangle contains 6 connected triples (3 choices of the middle node, 2 orders of the ends), and it also produces 6 closed walks of length 3 (3 starting nodes, 2 directions), which is why \(\operatorname{trace}A^3=6\triangle_G\). Node \(j\) is the middle of \(d_j(d_j-1)\) ordered triples, so the number of connected triples is \(\sum_jd_j(d_j-1)=d^Td-2L\). In walk language this is the number of two-hop walks, \(N_2=u^TA^2u=d^Td\), minus the closed ones that go out and come straight back, \(\operatorname{trace}A^2=2L\). Altogether</p>
<div class="formula">\[\check c_G=\frac{6\,\triangle_G}{N_2-\operatorname{trace}A^2}=\frac{\operatorname{trace}A^3}{d^Td-2L}=\frac{\sum_{k=1}^N\lambda_k^3}{\sum_{j=1}^N d_j(d_j-1)}.\]</div>
<h4>Why the two definitions differ</h4>
<p>The two numbers are both averages of the local coefficients, with different weights. Node \(j\) closes \(y_j=c_G(j)\tfrac{d_j(d_j-1)}2\) triangles and every triangle is counted at its three corners, so \(6\triangle_G=2\sum_jy_j=\sum_j d_j(d_j-1)\,c_G(j)\). Dividing by the number of triples:</p>
<div class="formula">\[\check c_G=\frac{\sum_j d_j(d_j-1)\,c_G(j)}{\sum_j d_j(d_j-1)}\qquad\text{versus}\qquad c_G=\frac1N\sum_j c_G(j).\]</div>
<p>The graph clustering coefficient weights every node equally; the transitivity weights each node by its number of triples, so high-degree nodes dominate it. A graph made of many small triangles attached to one hub has \(c_G\gt\check c_G\): most nodes have \(c=1\), but the hub, with many unlinked neighbour pairs, drags the transitivity down. A dense core with many pendant nodes has the opposite, because the pendant nodes count as \(c=0\) in the plain average but carry no triples.</p>
<voice r="2" t="35:18"><p>He calls the transitivity mathematically superior to the intuitive definition, because it is written in walk counts (\(W_3=\operatorname{trace}A^3\)) and therefore in eigenvalues. The intuitive version fits a social reading: an important person has many friends who also know each other. The annoying part is that the two definitions do not always give the same number, so which node looks most important can depend on the choice. He had never seen the two compared systematically and suggests a scatter plot of one against the other over many graphs; what in a graph makes them differ is, for him, an open question.</p></voice>
<p>A worked example, \(K_4\) minus the link (1,4): the degrees are \((2,3,3,2)\) and there are 2 triangles. Nodes 1 and 4 have their two neighbours linked, \(c=1\); nodes 2 and 3 have three neighbours with two links among them, \(c=\tfrac23\). Then \(c_G=\tfrac14(1+1+\tfrac23+\tfrac23)=\tfrac56\), while \(\check c_G=\tfrac{6\cdot2}{2+6+6+2}=\tfrac{12}{16}=\tfrac34\). The weighted average gives the same \(\tfrac{2\cdot1+2\cdot1+6\cdot\frac23+6\cdot\frac23}{16}=\tfrac34\). Reference values: \(K_N\) has \(c_G=\check c_G=1\); trees and all bipartite graphs have no triangles and clustering 0; in a random graph with link probability \(p\) the clustering is \(p\) (lecture 4).</p>
<div class="note">Exam prep Q3 asks for \(\check c_G\) of \(K_4\setminus(1,4)\): \(\tfrac{12}{16}=\tfrac34\). Homework 1 A.1(c): one triangle \(\{2,3,4\}\), \(c_G=\tfrac{5}{21}\) and \(\check c_G=\tfrac{6}{40-16}=\tfrac14\). Always state which definition you are using.</div>`},
{id:'l2s3',title:'Walks, paths, hopcount and diameter',body:R`
<p>Transport in a network follows routes, and the length of those routes is measured in hops. A <dfn>walk</dfn> of length \(k\) from \(i\) to \(j\) is a sequence of \(k\) links \(n_0\to n_1\to\dots\to n_k\) with \(n_0=i\) and \(n_k=j\), where nodes may repeat. A path is a walk in which all nodes are different.</p>
<h4>Counting walks with powers of \(A\)</h4>
<p>The powers of the adjacency matrix count walks. The entry \((A^2)_{ij}=\sum_m a_{im}a_{mj}\) adds a one for every node \(m\) adjacent to both \(i\) and \(j\), so it counts the walks of two hops from \(i\) to \(j\). Repeating the argument, \((A^k)_{ij}=\sum_m(A^{k-1})_{im}\,a_{mj}\) extends every \((k-1)\)-hop walk that ends at a neighbour \(m\) of \(j\) by the final hop, so</p>
<div class="formula">\[\begin{aligned}(A^k)_{ij}&=\#\{k\text{-hop walks from } i \text{ to } j\},\\ N_k=u^TA^ku&=\#\{\text{all }k\text{-hop walks}\},\qquad W_k=\operatorname{trace}A^k=\sum_{j=1}^N\lambda_j^k.\end{aligned}\]</div>
<p>\(W_k\) counts closed walks, which start and end at the same node. The small cases are familiar: \(N_1=W_2=2L\), \(N_2=d^Td\), \(W_3=6\triangle_G\). The last equality, \(\operatorname{trace}A^k=\sum_j\lambda_j^k\), links walks to eigenvalues and is used heavily in lecture 3.</p>
<voice r="2" t="42:39" hint><p>This is the formula he singles out as the take-away of the lecture: \((A^k)_{ij}\) is the number of \(k\)-hop walks from \(i\) to \(j\). Write it on your formula sheet and remember it. Mind the word <em>walks</em>; he corrects himself on this in class. The powers of \(A\) count walks, not paths. Counting \(k\)-hop paths from \(A\) means excluding repeated nodes, which turns it into a combinatorial problem that was still open (a PhD student of his was working on it). Two problems that look almost the same can be easy and very hard.</p></voice>
<h4>Shortest paths, hopcount and the distance matrix</h4>
<p>In a weighted graph each link carries a weight \(w_l\) (distance, delay, cost) and the weight of a path is \(w(P)=\sum_{l\in P}w_l\); other choices exist, such as the bottleneck capacity \(\min_{l\in P}w_l\). With all weights equal to one, the weight of a path is its number of hops. The <dfn>hopcount</dfn> \(H_{ij}\) is the number of hops of the shortest path from \(i\) to \(j\). Collecting all hopcounts gives the <dfn>distance matrix</dfn> \(H\), which behaves like a distance: \(H_{ij}\ge0\), \(H_{ii}=0\), and the triangle inequality \(H_{ik}+H_{kj}\ge H_{ij}\) holds, because going via \(k\) can never beat the shortest route. For the running example,</p>
<div class="formula">\[H=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&1&2&2&1\\1&0&1&2&1&1\\1&1&0&1&2&2\\2&2&1&0&1&2\\2&1&2&1&0&1\\1&1&2&2&1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad H_{14}=2.\]</div>
<voice r="2" t="43:38"><p>On link weights: summing them is natural when the quantity is additive, like kilometres or travel time. For bandwidth in a communication network a path is only as good as its weakest link, so the path weight is the minimum. Realistic links carry a vector of weights (distance, time, tolls), and finding a route that meets all constraints is quality-of-service routing, which he mentions but leaves out of this course. He goes quickly over the distance matrix: the triangle inequality holds because \(H_{ij}\) is the length of the <em>shortest</em> path, which no detour can beat. The idea comes back in part two, where the effective graph resistance turns out to be a distance matrix as well (lectures 5 and 6).</p></voice>
<p>Two summary numbers come from \(H\). The <dfn>diameter</dfn> \(\rho\) is the largest entry, the hopcount of the longest shortest path; here \(\rho=2\). The <dfn>average hopcount</dfn> is the mean over all ordered pairs,</p>
<div class="formula">\[E[H]=\frac1{N(N-1)}\sum_{i\ne j}H_{ij},\]</div>
<p>which for the running example is \(\tfrac{42}{30}=\tfrac75\). It measures how efficiently the network transports items: the lower, the fewer hops a typical item needs.</p>
<h4>Reading the diameter from \(A\)</h4>
<p>Walks and shortest paths meet in a simple observation. For a fixed pair, \((A^k)_{ij}\) is zero for every \(k\lt H_{ij}\) and becomes positive at \(k=H_{ij}\). A shortest walk cannot revisit a node (otherwise the loop could be cut out to make it shorter), so it is a path, and \((A^{H_{ij}})_{ij}\) is the number of shortest paths between \(i\) and \(j\). Consequently the diameter is the smallest \(k\) for which every entry of \(I+A+A^2+\dots+A^k\) is positive. Choosing binomial weights turns this sum into a single power, giving a faster test: \(\rho\) is the smallest \(k\) for which \((I+A)^k\) has no zero entries. Two related bounds are good to know. If \(A\) has \(q\) distinct eigenvalues, then \(\rho\lt q\). And a node can reach at most \(d_{\max}\) nodes in one hop, \(d_{\max}(d_{\max}-1)\) new ones in two hops, and so on, which gives the <dfn>Moore bound</dfn>:</p>
<div class="formula">\[N\le1+d_{\max}\sum_{k=0}^{\rho-1}(d_{\max}-1)^k=1+d_{\max}\frac{(d_{\max}-1)^\rho-1}{d_{\max}-2}.\]</div>
<p>It holds with equality for the Petersen graph (\(d_{\max}=3,\ \rho=2,\ N=1+3+6=10\)). Read the other way, it says a graph with small degrees must have a large diameter.</p>
<fig id="l2_diam" src="L2 slide 29">Reading the diameter from powers of A: the first k for which all entries of I + A + … + Aᵏ are positive.</fig>
<voice r="2" t="64:47"><p>Why a sum and not just \(A^k\)? Think of a bipartite graph: every hop switches sides, so \(A^k\) stays zero for every pair whose distance has the wrong parity, however large \(k\) gets. Adding all powers (or the identity, in \((I+A)^k\)) removes the problem. His recipe: put ones on the diagonal of \(A\) and keep multiplying until no zero entry is left. In the running example \(I+A+A^2\) is already positive while \(A^2\) alone still has zeros, so \(\rho=2\). He also points out that the first \(k\) with \((A^k)_{ij}\gt0\) is the hopcount, so shortest paths can be found from walk counts alone, although running Dijkstra between all pairs is faster in practice.</p></voice>
<p>Milgram's 1967 experiment measured the average hopcount of a social network: letters forwarded only to personal acquaintances reached a stranger in about six steps ("six degrees of separation"). Repeated on today's online social networks, the number is even lower. Short average paths compared with the size of the network, \(E[H]=O(\log N)\), are called the small-world property (lecture 4).</p>
<voice r="2" t="70:01"><p>He points out that Milgram's letters only sampled a few of the shortest paths. Online social networks allow the exact computation over millions of people: Facebook's average hopcount was a little above 5 in 2008 and somewhat lower in 2010, a measurable sign that the world is getting smaller.</p></voice>
<div class="note">Exam prep Q4: in \(K_4\setminus(1,4)\) five pairs are at distance 1 and one at distance 2, so \(\rho=2\) and \(E[H]=\tfrac{5\cdot1+2}{6}=\tfrac76\). Homework 1 A.1(d): \(\rho=4\) (between nodes 4 and 7) and \(\bar h=\tfrac{78}{42}\approx1.86\).</div>`},
{id:'l2s4',title:'Betweenness and closeness',body:R`
<p>Degree says how many neighbours a node has, not how much traffic passes through it. Betweenness measures the latter. Imagine every pair of nodes sending one unit of traffic to each other along shortest paths. The <dfn>betweenness</dfn> of a node \(n\) (or a link \(l\)) is the total amount of that traffic passing through \(n\) (or over \(l\)). When a pair \(s,t\) has several shortest paths, its unit of traffic is split evenly over them, so each of the \(\sigma_{st}\) shortest paths carries \(1/\sigma_{st}\):</p>
<div class="formula">\[B_n=\sum_{s\lt t,\ s,t\ne n}\frac{\sigma_{st}(n)}{\sigma_{st}},\]</div>
<p>where \(\sigma_{st}(n)\) is the number of shortest paths from \(s\) to \(t\) that pass through \(n\). Link betweenness is defined the same way with paths that traverse the link.</p>
<voice r="2" t="73:23"><p>Why betweenness matters, according to the lecture: attacks. Remove links in order of decreasing betweenness (the highest first, then the next) and many networks fall apart after close to the minimum possible number of removals. The \(1/\sigma_{st}\) split is an agreement; counting every shortest path as a whole one is the alternative, and neither is perfectly fair. In real weighted networks two equally short paths almost never occur, but a program must still handle the case. His advice: program the betweenness yourself once to get a feel for what it does.</p></voice>
<p>Betweenness and hopcount are tied together by a sum rule. The unit of traffic of pair \((s,t)\) crosses \(H_{st}\) links and passes through \(H_{st}-1\) intermediate nodes. Adding over all \(\binom N2\) pairs,</p>
<div class="formula">\[\sum_{\text{links }l}B_l=\sum_{s\lt t}H_{st}=\binom N2E[H],\qquad\sum_{\text{nodes }n}B_n=\binom N2\big(E[H]-1\big).\]</div>
<p>So the average link betweenness is \(\binom N2E[H]/L\). A quick check on \(K_4\setminus(1,4)\): \(E[H]=\tfrac76\), so the node betweenness adds up to \(6\cdot\tfrac16=1\). Indeed only the pair (1,4) needs an intermediate node, and its two shortest paths, via 2 and via 3, give \(B_2=B_3=\tfrac12\). (The variance of the betweenness is an open problem.)</p>
<fig id="l2_betwtab" src="L2 slide 34">Node and link betweenness with the 1/k rule for pairs with k shortest paths.</fig>
<h4>Closeness</h4>
<p>Where betweenness counts how often a node lies <em>on</em> shortest paths, <dfn>closeness</dfn> measures how short the paths <em>from</em> the node are. It is the reciprocal of the farness, the total hopcount to all other nodes:</p>
<div class="formula">\[C_i=\frac1{\sum_{j\ne i}H_{ij}}.\]</div>
<p>The farness is a row sum of the distance matrix. In the running example the row sums are \((7,6,7,8,7,7)\), so node 2 is the most central by closeness, \(C_2=\tfrac16\). Lecture 6 compares closeness, which only uses shortest paths, with a flow-based measure that uses all paths.</p>
<p>The two centralities need not agree. In Homework 1 A.1 the degrees are \(d=(2,3,3,2,3,2,1)\), the closeness is \(C=(\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{13},\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{10},\tfrac1{15})\) and the betweenness is \(b=(3,3,3,0,6,3,0)\). Node 5 has by far the largest betweenness, because it is the only gateway to node 7, but on closeness it ties with four other nodes.</p>
<h4>Betweenness in \(K_{m,n}\) and its line graph</h4>
<p>A node in the group of size \(m\) of \(K_{m,n}\) lies on shortest paths only between pairs in the other group. Each such pair is at distance 2 with \(m\) shortest paths, one through every node of the \(m\)-group, so each pair contributes \(\tfrac1m\), and there are \(\binom n2\) pairs: \(B=\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2m}\). The largest node betweenness occurs for the most unbalanced split, the star, whose centre lies on the single shortest path of every pair of leaves, \(\binom{N-1}2\). In the line graph \(l(K_{m,n})\) every node has betweenness \(\tfrac{(m-1)(n-1)}2\), which is largest for the balanced split \(m=\lfloor N/2\rfloor\).</p>
<div class="note">Homework 1 A.1(e)–(f) asks whether betweenness and closeness pick the same most central node (they do not). Exercise session 1, Problem 4(c)–(d) derives the \(K_{m,n}\) results above. Exam prep Q5 computes betweenness in a line graph.</div>`},
{id:'l2s5',title:'Connectivity and Menger’s theorem',body:R`
<p>A graph is <dfn>connected</dfn> if there is a path between every pair of nodes. Connectivity metrics ask how robust that property is: how much damage does it take to break it? The <dfn>edge connectivity</dfn> \(\lambda(G)\) is the minimum number of links whose removal disconnects the graph, and the <dfn>vertex connectivity</dfn> \(\kappa(G)\) is the minimum number of nodes. For \(N\gt2\) they satisfy the chain</p>
<div class="formula">\[\kappa(G)\ \le\ \lambda(G)\ \le\ d_{\min}(G)\ \le\ \frac{2L}N.\]</div>
<p>Each step has a short reason. Removing all \(d_{\min}\) links of a node of minimum degree isolates it, so \(\lambda\le d_{\min}\). For every link of a minimum link cut, removing one of its end points (chosen so as not to remove all of one side) also destroys that link, so at most \(\lambda\) node removals suffice, \(\kappa\le\lambda\). And the minimum degree cannot exceed the average degree \(2L/N\). In the slide example the two dense halves share the single node C: \(\kappa=1\), \(\lambda=2\), \(d_{\min}=3\).</p>
<fig id="l2_menger" src="L2 slide 38">Menger’s theorem and the inequality κ(G) ≤ λ(G) ≤ d_min.</fig>
<p><dfn>Menger's theorem</dfn> turns these cut numbers into a statement about routes. The maximum number of link-disjoint paths between two nodes \(A\) and \(B\) (paths that share no link) equals the minimum number of links whose removal separates \(A\) from \(B\), and the same holds for node-disjoint paths and node removals. As a consequence, every pair of nodes is joined by at least \(\lambda(G)\) link-disjoint and at least \(\kappa(G)\) node-disjoint paths. That is the robustness interpretation: a network with \(\lambda(G)=2\) survives any single link failure, because every pair has a second route that does not share a link with the first.</p>
<voice r="2" t="91:49"><p>The practical side of Menger's theorem, as told in class: optical communication networks keep a link-disjoint backup path ready for each connection. When a link fails, because of an earthquake or another disaster in a region, the traffic flips to the backup within roughly 50 ms, which you may notice as a short hiccup. A designer who wants one, two or three backups computes the connectivity to check that the network allows it.</p></voice>
<p>A neat fact about the complement: <strong>if \(G\) is disconnected, then \(G^c\) is connected.</strong> Take two nodes \(i\) and \(j\). If they lie in different components of \(G\), they are not linked in \(G\), so they are linked in \(G^c\). If they lie in the same component, pick a node \(m\) in another component; neither \(i\) nor \(j\) is linked to \(m\) in \(G\), so \(i\sim m\sim j\) is a path in \(G^c\). The converse is false: a graph and its complement can both be connected, as for the ring \(C_5\), which is its own complement.</p>
<voice r="2" t="93:54" hint><p>He shows this proof because he finds it elegant, not to have it memorised, and in 2022 he said the exam was open book. The current exam-prep set says closed book with a formula sheet you prepare yourself, so understand the argument and put the result on your sheet.</p></voice>
<div class="note">Exam prep Q10: in \(K_4\setminus(1,4)\) the minimum degree is 2 and removing any single link leaves it connected, so 2 links must be removed. The algebraic connectivity of lecture 3, \(a(G)=2\), is a spectral measure of the same robustness.</div>`},
{id:'l2s6',title:'Degree assortativity and degree-preserving rewiring',body:R`
<p>Do hubs connect to hubs, or to low-degree nodes? Degree assortativity answers this with a correlation coefficient. Recall that for two random variables the linear correlation coefficient</p>
<div class="formula">\[\rho(X,Y)=\frac{E[XY]-E[X]E[Y]}{\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}[X]\operatorname{Var}[Y]}}\in[-1,1]\]</div>
<p>is 0 without linear relation and \(\pm1\) when \(Y\) is an exact linear function of \(X\). The <dfn>degree assortativity</dfn> \(\rho_D\) applies this to the degrees at the two ends of a link: pick a link at random, look at it from one end \(l^+\) and the other \(l^-\), and correlate \(D_{l^+}\) with \(D_{l^-}\). A network is assortative if \(\rho_D\gt0\) (like attracts like, typical of social networks) and disassortative if \(\rho_D\lt0\) (hubs attach to low-degree nodes, typical of the Internet and of biological networks).</p>
<voice r="2" t="79:09"><p>He stresses that the correlation is taken between two nodes that are <em>connected</em>: the two ends of a link, not two random nodes. His examples: people linked according to their wealth form an assortative network (the rich mostly know the rich), while the craftsmen of a village form a disassortative one (the shoemaker deals with the baker, not with another shoemaker, since he makes his own shoes). On correlation coefficients in general: a linear relation can be tested exactly, so if you expect a parabola or a logarithm, transform the data first (square or log) and check for a straight line rather than fitting a curve. The walk formula below is the version you can program straight from \(A\).</p></voice>
<h4>From the definition to the walk formula</h4>
<p>Every moment needed can be written with walk counts \(N_k=u^TA^ku\). Count every link in both orientations, \(2L=N_1\) oriented links in total. The end \(l^+\) of a random oriented link sits at node \(i\) with probability \(d_i/N_1\) (a node with more links is the end of more of them), so</p>
<div class="formula">\[\begin{aligned}E[D_{l^+}]&=\sum_i\frac{d_i}{N_1}\,d_i=\frac{d^Td}{N_1}=\frac{N_2}{N_1},\qquad E[D_{l^+}^2]=\frac{\sum_id_i^3}{N_1},\\ E[D_{l^+}D_{l^-}]&=\frac{\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}d_id_j}{N_1}=\frac{d^TAd}{N_1}=\frac{N_3}{N_1},\end{aligned}\]</div>
<p>where the last step uses \(d=Au\), so \(d^TAd=u^TA^3u\). By symmetry \(D_{l^-}\) has the same mean and variance. Substituting into the correlation coefficient and multiplying numerator and denominator by \(N_1^2\):</p>
<div class="formula">\[\rho_D=\frac{N_3/N_1-(N_2/N_1)^2}{\sum_id_i^3/N_1-(N_2/N_1)^2}=\frac{N_1N_3-N_2^2}{N_1\sum_{j=1}^Nd_j^3-N_2^2}.\]</div>
<p>Two checks. In a star every link joins the centre (degree \(N-1\)) to a leaf (degree 1), so one end is high exactly when the other is low and \(\rho_D=-1\). In a regular graph all degrees are equal, the variance is zero, and \(\rho_D\) is undefined (0/0).</p>
<fig id="l2_dpr" src="L2 slide 45">Degree-preserving rewiring changes ρ_D (and the spectrum) without touching any degree.</fig>
<h4>Degree-preserving rewiring</h4>
<p>Sometimes the degrees of a network are fixed (a router has a fixed number of ports) but the wiring can be changed. <dfn>Degree-preserving rewiring</dfn> takes two links \(a\!-\!b\) and \(c\!-\!d\) and replaces them by \(a\!-\!c\) and \(b\!-\!d\). Every node keeps its degree, so \(N_1\), \(N_2=d^Td\) and \(\sum_jd_j^3\) do not change; only \(N_3=2\sum_{\text{links}}d_id_j\) does, and in it only the two rewired terms change:</p>
<div class="formula">\[\Delta N_3=2\big[(d_ad_c+d_bd_d)-(d_ad_b+d_cd_d)\big]=2(d_a-d_d)(d_c-d_b).\]</div>
<p>So \(\rho_D\) moves linearly with \(N_3\), and a rewiring step raises the assortativity exactly when it pairs high degrees with high degrees. Repeating such steps steers \(\rho_D\) towards its graph-specific maximum (which is below 1, since the degrees constrain what is possible) or minimum; greedy steering can get stuck in local maxima. Rewiring changes other properties too: higher assortativity increases the spectral radius \(\lambda_1\) and can split the network into disconnected components (lecture 3).</p>
<voice r="2" t="83:40"><p>The motivation came from routers: a router has a fixed number of ports, so the degrees are fixed, and the question is whether rewiring alone can improve the network. Airlines with a fixed fleet face the same question, and he speculates that biology may use the same economy, because rewiring existing relations is cheaper than creating new elements. The greedy procedure in class: pick two links, try the possible rewirings, keep one only if \(\rho_D\) goes up, and repeat until you reach a (local) maximum. He admits it looks difficult at first sight, "but at least look at it", and announces that the spectral radius reacts strongly to rewiring even though no degree changes (lecture 3).</p></voice>`},
{id:'l2s7',title:'Modularity and community detection',body:R`
<p>Many networks consist of groups that are densely connected inside and sparsely connected to each other: departments in a company, functional modules in the brain. Finding these groups is called partitioning, clustering or community detection. The difficulty is that "natural cluster" has no agreed definition, so the problem is ill-posed; Szemerédi's regularity lemma even shows that every large graph can be cut into almost equal parts with links between parts that look random. The two main families of methods are modularity maximisation and spectral clustering (the Fiedler vector of lecture 3).</p>
<h4>The random baseline</h4>
<p>To call a group dense we need something to compare with. Keep every node's degree but place the links at random: node \(i\) has \(d_i\) link ends, and each of them lands on node \(j\) with probability about \(d_j/2L\), the share of all \(2L\) link ends that belong to \(j\). The expected number of links between \(i\) and \(j\) is therefore about \(\tfrac{d_id_j}{2L}\). This approximation can overshoot for hubs: with \(d_i=d_j=4\) and \(L=8\) it gives exactly 1, whereas the exact probability, computed by counting the graphs with those degrees, is \(\tfrac{96}{97}\).</p>
<h4>Newman's modularity</h4>
<p><dfn>Newman's modularity</dfn> compares, for a given partition into clusters \(C_1,\dots,C_c\), the actual links inside clusters with this baseline:</p>
<div class="formula">\[m=\frac1{2L}\sum_{i=1}^N\sum_{j=1}^N\left(a_{ij}-\frac{d_id_j}{2L}\right)1_{\{i,j\text{ in the same cluster}\}}=\sum_{k=1}^c\left[\frac{L_k}{L}-\left(\frac{D_k}{2L}\right)^2\right].\]</div>
<p>The second form follows by summing per cluster: inside cluster \(k\) the \(a_{ij}\) add up to \(2L_k\), twice its number of internal links, and the \(d_id_j\) add up to \(D_k^2\), the square of the total degree \(D_k\) of the cluster. Each cluster contributes the fraction of links it holds minus the fraction expected by chance. Putting all nodes in one cluster gives \(L_1=L\), \(D_1=2L\) and \(m=0\), as it should; positive values mean more internal links than chance. Example: two triangles joined by one link (\(N=6,\ L=7\)), split into the two triangles. Each cluster has \(L_k=3\) and \(D_k=2+2+3=7\), so \(m=2\left[\tfrac37-\left(\tfrac7{14}\right)^2\right]=\tfrac67-\tfrac12=\tfrac5{14}\approx0.36\).</p>
<p>In matrix form, with the <dfn>modularity matrix</dfn> \(M=A-\tfrac{dd^T}{2L}\) and the \(N\times c\) community matrix \(S\) (\(s_{ik}=1\) if node \(i\) is in cluster \(k\)), \(m=\tfrac1{2L}\operatorname{trace}(S^TMS)\). Finding the partition with the largest modularity is NP-hard; practical algorithms are heuristics, and spectral methods use the eigenvectors of \(M\).</p>
<fig id="l2_modularity" src="L2 slide 48">Newman’s modularity: links inside clusters minus the random-placement baseline d_i d_j / 2L.</fig>
<div class="note lec">Modularity was not discussed in the 2022 recording of this lecture, which ends after connectivity and assortativity. It is on the current slides, so treat it as course material, but there are no notes from class for this part.</div>`},
{id:'l2s8',title:'More centralities: Katz, coreness and the friendship paradox',body:R`
<p>The slides close with a few further metrics. They are less central for the exam (and were not covered in the 2022 recording), but each says something useful about walks or degrees.</p>
<h4>Katz centrality</h4>
<p>Degree counts the walks of length 1 from a node. <dfn>Katz centrality</dfn> counts walks of every length, giving a walk of length \(k\) the weight \(\alpha^k\) so that long walks count less:</p>
<div class="formula">\[\kappa_i=\sum_{k=1}^\infty\alpha^k(A^ku)_i=\Big(\big((I-\alpha A)^{-1}-I\big)u\Big)_i,\qquad 0\lt\alpha\lt\frac1{\lambda_1}.\]</div>
<p>The closed form is a geometric series of matrices, which converges only if \(\alpha\lambda_1\lt1\), with \(\lambda_1\) the largest eigenvalue of \(A\). For small \(\alpha\) the first term dominates and \(\kappa_i/\alpha\to d_i\): Katz centrality reduces to the degree. In dense large graphs this happens automatically, because \(\lambda_1\) grows and forces \(\alpha\) to be small.</p>
<h4>The friendship paradox</h4>
<p>"Your friends have, on average, more friends than you." Averaged over all nodes, the mean degree of a node's neighbours is at least the mean degree:</p>
<div class="formula">\[\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N\frac1{d_i}\sum_{j=1}^Na_{ij}d_j\ \ge\ \frac1N\sum_{i=1}^Nd_i.\]</div>
<p>The proof uses symmetry. Swapping the roles of \(i\) and \(j\) shows that \(\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\tfrac{d_j}{d_i}=\tfrac12\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\left(\tfrac{d_j}{d_i}+\tfrac{d_i}{d_j}\right)\), while \(\sum_id_i=\tfrac12\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\cdot2\). The difference of the two sides, multiplied by \(N\), is therefore \(\tfrac12\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}\left(\sqrt{d_j/d_i}-\sqrt{d_i/d_j}\right)^2\ge0\). Equality holds only when every link joins two nodes of equal degree, as in a regular graph, so the gap is a measure of irregularity. The quantity \(V(G)=\sum_{\text{links}}\left(\tfrac{d_i}{d_j}+\tfrac{d_j}{d_i}\right)\) of Homework 1 A.2(c) is this same sum, and it equals \(2L\) exactly for regular graphs. In a random graph a random neighbour has expected degree \(1+(N-2)p\), one more than the average (lecture 4).</p>
<h4>Coreness</h4>
<p>The <dfn>\(k\)-core</dfn> of a graph is the largest subgraph in which every node has at least \(k\) neighbours inside the subgraph. It is found by repeatedly removing every node of degree less than \(k\) until none is left; removing a node lowers the degrees of its neighbours, which may then have to go as well. The coreness of a node is the largest \(k\) for which it survives. Adding or removing one link changes any coreness by at most 1, and a node of coreness \(c\) has at least \(c\) neighbours of coreness \(c\) or higher. Coreness turned out to identify influential spreaders better than degree: a hub at the edge of the network spreads less than a moderately connected node deep in the core.</p>
<fig id="l2_kcore" src="L2 slide 65">k-core decomposition: recursively remove nodes of degree below k.</fig>
<h4>Combining metrics</h4>
<p>To score a property such as robustness, which has no single definition, the R-model combines metrics linearly, \(R=s^Tt\), with a vector \(t\) of graph metrics and a vector \(s\) of weights chosen for the service considered. After normalisation \(R=\cos\theta\) lies between 0 and 1. The weak point is that almost all metrics are correlated with each other, so they are not independent axes, and the weights are chosen by hand.</p>`}
],
defs:[
{t:'Graph metric',d:R`Quantity that measures a graph property, at node, link or graph level; local or global; centrality metrics rank importance.`},
{t:'Average degree',d:R`\(d_{av}=E[D]=2L/N\); bounds \(2-2/N\le d_{av}\le N-1\) for connected \(G\).`},
{t:'Basic law of the degree',d:R`\(\sum_j d_j=u^Td=u^TAu=2L\); number of odd-degree nodes is even.`},
{t:'Degree distribution',d:R`\(\Pr[D=k]\): fraction of nodes with degree \(k\); its mean is \(2L/N\).`},
{t:'Graphical sequence',d:R`Integer vector realisable as the degree sequence of a simple graph (Erdős–Gallai, Havel–Hakimi tests).`},
{t:'Clustering coefficient \\(c_G(v)\\)',d:R`\(2y/(d_v(d_v-1))\), fraction of linked neighbour pairs; \(c_G\) is the average over nodes.`},
{t:'Transitivity \\(\\check c_G\\)',d:R`\(6\triangle/(d^Td-2L)=\operatorname{trace}A^3/\sum_j d_j(d_j-1)\); the average of \(c_G(j)\) weighted by \(d_j(d_j-1)\); equals 1 for \(K_N\).`},
{t:'Walk / path',d:R`Walk: sequence of \(k\) links; path: walk with all nodes distinct. \((A^k)_{ij}\) counts \(k\)-hop walks \(i\to j\).`},
{t:'Hopcount \\(H_{ij}\\)',d:R`Number of links of a shortest path from \(i\) to \(j\); distance matrix obeys the triangle inequality.`},
{t:'Distance matrix \\(H\\)',d:R`Matrix of hopcounts: non-negative, zero diagonal, triangle inequality; row sums are the farness.`},
{t:'Diameter \\(\\rho\\)',d:R`Hopcount of the longest shortest path; smallest \(k\) such that \((I+A)^k\) has all entries positive.`},
{t:'Average hopcount \\(E[H]\\)',d:R`Mean hopcount over all node pairs, \(\tfrac1{N(N-1)}\sum_{i\ne j}H_{ij}\); measures transport efficiency.`},
{t:'Moore bound',d:R`\(N\le1+d_{\max}\sum_{k=0}^{\rho-1}(d_{\max}-1)^k\); equality for the Petersen graph.`},
{t:'Betweenness \\(B_n, B_l\\)',d:R`Number of shortest paths through node \(n\) (link \(l\)); a pair with \(k\) shortest paths contributes \(1/k\) each. \(\sum_lB_l=\binom N2E[H]\).`},
{t:'Closeness \\(C_i\\)',d:R`\(C_i=1/\sum_{j\ne i}H_{ij}\), reciprocal of the farness.`},
{t:'Connected graph',d:R`A path exists between every pair of nodes; equivalently \(a(G)=\mu_{N-1}\gt0\).`},
{t:'Edge connectivity \\(\\lambda(G)\\)',d:R`Minimum number of links whose removal disconnects \(G\); \(\kappa(G)\le\lambda(G)\le d_{\min}\).`},
{t:'Vertex connectivity \\(\\kappa(G)\\)',d:R`Minimum number of nodes whose removal disconnects \(G\).`},
{t:'Menger’s theorem',d:R`Max number of link-(node-)disjoint paths between two nodes equals the min number of links (nodes) separating them.`},
{t:'Degree assortativity \\(\\rho_D\\)',d:R`Linear correlation of degrees at both ends of a link; \(\rho_D=(N_1N_3-N_2^2)/(N_1\sum d_j^3-N_2^2)\).`},
{t:'Degree-preserving rewiring',d:R`Swap end points of two links; degrees unchanged, only \(N_3\) changes, by \(2(d_a-d_d)(d_c-d_b)\).`},
{t:'Modularity \\(m\\)',d:R`\(\frac1{2L}\sum_c\sum_{i,j\in c}(a_{ij}-d_id_j/2L)=\sum_k[L_k/L-(D_k/2L)^2]\); links inside clusters minus random expectation.`},
{t:'Modularity matrix \\(M\\)',d:R`\(M=A-dd^T/(2L)\); \(m=\operatorname{trace}(S^TMS)/(2L)\).`},
{t:'Katz centrality',d:R`\(\kappa_i=\sum_{k\ge1}\alpha^k(A^ku)_i\), weighted count of walks from \(i\); requires \(\alpha\lt1/\lambda_1\); tends to the degree for small \(\alpha\).`},
{t:'\\(k\\)-core / coreness',d:R`Largest subgraph in which every node has degree \(\ge k\); coreness of a node is the largest \(k\) of a core containing it.`},
{t:'Friendship paradox',d:R`Average degree of a node's neighbours is at least the average degree; equality only for regular graphs.`}
]});
